WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.959
I want to depress you just a bit,
basically just to remind you how bad

00:00:03.959 --> 00:00:07.560
things are, and how little things have
actually changed since one of the

00:00:07.560 --> 00:00:12.450
biggest crises of all time—the second
biggest financial crisis, really, the one

00:00:12.450 --> 00:00:18.690
that began in 2007-2008. And so I'm going to just very quickly depress you a bit

00:00:18.690 --> 00:00:22.529
but I promise I will get to hopeful,
positive things towards the end. And we

00:00:22.529 --> 00:00:28.680
have a huge amount of time. So, four big
fat problems. The first I'll call the

00:00:28.680 --> 00:00:32.550
financial problem. The finance problem.
And this is, really what I mean by this

00:00:32.550 --> 00:00:36.270
is the problem within the financial
sector—and I'm going to be a bit

00:00:36.270 --> 00:00:39.390
superficial here because I'm going to go
through lots of both economic thinking

00:00:39.390 --> 00:00:42.450
and also economic history—but what I
mean by this is a kind of

00:00:42.450 --> 00:00:47.700
financialization of finance. In other
words, finance is financing finance. And

00:00:47.700 --> 00:00:51.360
what you see here in this curve—this is
for the UK economy, but the US economy

00:00:51.360 --> 00:00:56.940
looks quite similar—the degree to which
the financial sector has outpaced in its

00:00:56.940 --> 00:01:00.930
size the rest of the economy. So this is
looking at financial intermediation,

00:01:00.930 --> 00:01:05.339
which is basically banks but also whole
shadow banking system in all forms of

00:01:05.339 --> 00:01:09.060
different types of financial
institutions growing much faster than

00:01:09.060 --> 00:01:12.600
the rest of the economy. And basically
this is because so much of the financial

00:01:12.600 --> 00:01:16.830
sector has ended up just financing
finance, insurance and real estate, which

00:01:16.830 --> 00:01:23.640
conveniently, the acronym is FIRE. And
so, you know, this often makes people

00:01:23.640 --> 00:01:27.689
think, 'Oh god, this is terrible,' we need to
kind of definancialize and kind of fuel

00:01:27.689 --> 00:01:31.890
industry, right? So this is also why in
some countries, industrial policy has

00:01:31.890 --> 00:01:35.159
come back, and the idea that we need to
kind of fuel the makers. So these are the

00:01:35.159 --> 00:01:39.060
takers. And unfortunately, this brings me
to the second problem, which is that

00:01:39.060 --> 00:01:45.060
industry has become very financialized
itself. And by that I mean that

00:01:45.060 --> 00:01:52.380
increasingly in many large companies, and this is data here for 466 of the S&amp;P 500

00:01:52.380 --> 00:01:57.750
companies, the profits being earned are
not actually being reinvested back into

00:01:57.750 --> 00:02:02.100
production, back into the workforce—
human capital and skill, research and

00:02:02.100 --> 00:02:05.759
development—but used for exactly the
kind of things that Larry Fink was in

00:02:05.759 --> 00:02:10.410
theory warning about. So things
like share buybacks. And so this data

00:02:10.410 --> 00:02:13.470
here shows you that between 2008, so
after

00:02:13.470 --> 00:02:22.530
financial crisis, to 2017, these 466 big
companies spent over four trillion

00:02:22.530 --> 00:02:27.480
dollars in share buybacks, which was
equivalent to 53 percent of their

00:02:27.480 --> 00:02:30.540
profits, along with 3.1 trillion as dividends. And this would be

00:02:30.540 --> 00:02:34.380
fine as long as they were also again reinvesting back in. But that

00:02:34.380 --> 00:02:38.360
yellow bit there, which is the
reinvestment back into production,

00:02:38.360 --> 00:02:43.980
including skills, has been falling. And
you see, this by the way also at the

00:02:43.980 --> 00:02:48.060
macroeconomic level, you might know that GDP, gross domestic product, which is how

00:02:48.060 --> 00:02:52.380
we measure output of the economy, can be broken down in different ways. One of it

00:02:52.380 --> 00:02:56.370
is on the spending of all the different
components of the economy, so government

00:02:56.370 --> 00:03:00.720
spending (G), business spending (I),
consumption spending and then exports.

00:03:00.720 --> 00:03:05.220
And the I part, business investment, has
been falling. And this is not a

00:03:05.220 --> 00:03:08.390
coincidence, right? This is precisely
because there hasn't been that

00:03:08.390 --> 00:03:11.910
reinvestment. I'm just going to say one
thing because I often forget to say it. And

00:03:11.910 --> 00:03:16.590
apologies if I often do these little
parentheses, but this is a huge problem

00:03:16.590 --> 00:03:22.410
and this basically is much more of a problem than the so-called 'the

00:03:22.410 --> 00:03:26.430
robots are taking our jobs.' It's very
interesting if you look back at the

00:03:26.430 --> 00:03:30.269
history of economic thought, and I'll be
going through quite a bit of history in

00:03:30.269 --> 00:03:35.400
just a minute, David Ricardo one of the
first economists ever in 1821

00:03:35.400 --> 00:03:40.980
wrote a book called Principles of
Political Economy and Chapter 31—don't

00:03:40.980 --> 00:03:44.100
ask me how I remember these details, I
can't remember what I did this morning—

00:03:44.100 --> 00:03:49.440
but chapter 31 of this book that he
wrote in 1821 called 'On Machinery,' he

00:03:49.440 --> 00:03:53.760
warned that this rise and machines were
displacing labor. He was very worried

00:03:53.760 --> 00:03:57.720
about the effect that the rise of the
machines—today's robots—would be having

00:03:57.720 --> 00:04:02.580
on employment and wages. But what we
ended up having for basically 200 years,

00:04:02.580 --> 00:04:07.140
and it kind of stopped around the 1980s
and onwards, was that as long as profits

00:04:07.140 --> 00:04:10.890
were being reinvested back into the
economy it kind of didn't matter.

00:04:10.890 --> 00:04:14.790
Machines, yes, took jobs in one part of
the economy, but as long as profits were

00:04:14.790 --> 00:04:19.739
invested, they reappeared in other places. And skills were also formed as an

00:04:19.739 --> 00:04:24.000
endogenous function of that investment
process, right? Skills don't only come

00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:27.210
from government training programs, they

00:04:27.210 --> 00:04:29.970
also come from if you're kind of taken
care of inside the company, if you're

00:04:29.970 --> 00:04:34.080
invested in. And this is just really
important because there's so much talk

00:04:34.080 --> 00:04:37.259
today about the future of work and the
robots, what's going to happen, without

00:04:37.259 --> 00:04:41.669
actually putting these numbers in this
data at the center of the picture as

00:04:41.669 --> 00:04:45.750
just as important to confront. And this
is a corporate governance problem. So

00:04:45.750 --> 00:04:50.430
this was problem two—sorry, little
distraction there. Problem three is that

00:04:50.430 --> 00:04:56.280
in a time where we would really need
serious, ambitious policy—as ambitious as

00:04:56.280 --> 00:04:59.669
the kind of policies that got us the
welfare state, or that got a man to the

00:04:59.669 --> 00:05:04.199
moon and back again in one generation, we basically have states that have been

00:05:04.199 --> 00:05:09.240
convinced by, well, unfortunately, by
economists—we unfortunately have lots of

00:05:09.240 --> 00:05:13.320
influence—that at best, they can just
kind of tinker on the sidelines. You

00:05:13.320 --> 00:05:16.680
always have to first identify the market
failure, and then you're allowed to do

00:05:16.680 --> 00:05:20.940
something. And this kind of leads to this
very patchy view of what policy is for.

00:05:20.940 --> 00:05:26.159
So in a moment where we're calling for
things like a green transition, I'll get

00:05:26.159 --> 00:05:30.180
to this in a second, the fourth problem
is in fact climate change—when we need

00:05:30.180 --> 00:05:34.409
to—in theory, we're talking about it—
approach the sustainable development

00:05:34.409 --> 00:05:39.630
goals for real, where we have a rise in
inequality, at least relative inequality

00:05:39.630 --> 00:05:43.320
in many countries, and there's a talk
about reforming capitalism, this idea

00:05:43.320 --> 00:05:47.009
that you're just there patching up the
system and putting band-aids when it

00:05:47.009 --> 00:05:51.780
goes wrong is simply not going to get us
what we need. And it's not that these

00:05:51.780 --> 00:05:56.550
market failures don't exist. This is the
term that economists use, 'market failures,'

00:05:56.550 --> 00:05:59.099
and you always have to identify the
market failure before policy does

00:05:59.099 --> 00:06:03.509
anything. They do exist. So let me just
give you some examples here. When you

00:06:03.509 --> 00:06:07.590
have positive externalities, so goods
that create these really great spillovers,

00:06:07.590 --> 00:06:10.680
that it's hard to appropriate the
returns just for yourself, you will have

00:06:10.680 --> 00:06:14.699
under investment by the private sector
in that area. So government comes in and

00:06:14.699 --> 00:06:18.930
invests in things like basic research.
The opposite when businesses are

00:06:18.930 --> 00:06:22.919
producing too much of something. That is not good, a negative externality, then

00:06:22.919 --> 00:06:26.370
government comes in and does something like a carbon tax when you have

00:06:26.370 --> 00:06:29.909
asymmetric information. So cute little
companies don't get enough money because

00:06:29.909 --> 00:06:33.150
the banks don't actually know
anything about them, because they don't

00:06:33.150 --> 00:06:36.479
have enough history. Then you might need government to come in and do SME lending.

00:06:36.479 --> 00:06:41.129
So again, all these things happen, and this framework isn't

00:06:41.129 --> 00:06:45.149
completely useless. But in a time where
there's at least talk about

00:06:45.149 --> 00:06:50.009
rediscovering purpose in reforming
capitalism, and rethinking the direction,

00:06:50.009 --> 00:06:55.919
not just the rate, these tools are part
of the problem. And fourth, as I just kind

00:06:55.919 --> 00:06:59.969
of gave it away before, the climate
problem. This is kind of THE problem. If

00:06:59.969 --> 00:07:04.589
we don't sort this, everything else is
kind of irrelevant. We've got twelve

00:07:04.589 --> 00:07:08.189
years left, the IPCC report, if you
haven't read it, read it.

00:07:08.189 --> 00:07:13.559
Greta (Thunberg), young woman, reminds us that our house is literally on fire. Second time I

00:07:13.559 --> 00:07:16.949
use the word 'fire,' interesting you can
use it both for climate change and the

00:07:16.949 --> 00:07:21.990
financial sector, it's probably not a
coincidence. And you know, and

00:07:21.990 --> 00:07:25.949
unfortunately it's not just that there's
not enough spending on renewable energy,

00:07:25.949 --> 00:07:30.360
this also is data from the IPCC, so
apparently we're only spending about 20%

00:07:30.360 --> 00:07:33.569
of what we should be in terms of really
getting a renewable energy revolution

00:07:33.569 --> 00:07:37.169
going. But Green is not just about
renewable energy, it's really about

00:07:37.169 --> 00:07:41.909
transforming how our economies work. So getting some old manufacturing sectors

00:07:41.909 --> 00:07:46.469
to reduce their material content. And the
history of capitalism, we've often

00:07:46.469 --> 00:07:51.539
required real kind of bold demand side
policies, not just that supply push, and

00:07:51.539 --> 00:07:54.439
we're not getting those.

